Eq. (10) shows that an elemental component of _r_[2] ( _gij_ ( _r_ )-1) is transformed into a sin( _x_ )/ _x_ type damped oscillation in _Q_ -space. (The partial structure factor is then obtained as the superposition of such elemental oscillations.) The first maximum of sin( _x_ )/ _x_ (after _x_ = 0) is at x ≈ 7.72. Thus, the correlation length corresponding to _Q_ max, the FSDP peak position is defined as _r_ FSDP = 7.72/ _Q_ max (see also the Ehrenfest relation [72]). In our case _r_ FSDP ≈ 7.2 Å. This value is roughly equal to the double of the mean distance of centers of CS/ES tetrahedra. 

## Conclusions 

Short range order of Ge22Ga3Sb10S65 and Ge15Ga10Sb10S65 glasses was studied by neutron- and X-ray diffraction, combined with EXAFS measurements. The experimental data sets were fitted simultaneously by reverse Monte Carlo simulation technique. It has been established that Ge and Ga are both fourfold coordinated, while Sb and S atoms have 3 and 2 nearest neighbors, respectively.  The structure of the examined glasses can be described by the chemically ordered network model: Ge-S, Ga-S and Sb-S bonds are the most preferred. The typical bond lengths are 2.22 Å for Ge-S, 2.28 Å for Ga-S and 2.44 Å for Sb-S pairs, but some longer distances, most probably Sb-S pairs, in the 2.6 – 3 Å region were also observed. 

