## **RESULTS AND DISCUSSION** 

## **XANES Analysis** 

The room temperature normalized XANES spectra measured at Co K-edge are presented in Figs. 1(a, e) for the LSCNO and LCNO samples, respectively. The energy scale has been calibrated with the cobalt metalfoil by adjusting the Co K-edge at 7709 eV and utilizing a maximum in the first-order derivative of _µ_ . For further analysis, we perform a linear baseline correction in the pre-edge range and a spline baseline correction to the post-edge range in the Athena software [45, 48]. These baseline-corrected data were normalized to unity by using a point 800 eV away from the absorption K-edge of Co, similar to the ref. [36]. The XANES of these samples exhibit eight distinct observable features highlighted by the alphabets A–H in Figs. 1(a, e). The origin of first two features A and B have discrepancies in the literature and several reports have accredited them to the quadrupole transition between Co 1 _s→_ 3 _d_ , which were ascribed as the presence of eg and t2g states, respectively [36, 37, 49]. However, in a centrosymmetric environment, the 1 _s→_ eg and 1 _s→_ t2g transitions are not allowed in the dipole approximation (∆ _l_ = _±_ 1), though they are allowed in the quadrupolar transitions because of the hybridization between 4 _p_ and 3 _d_ orbitals [49–51]. Interestingly, the recent studies following the charge-transfer multiplet calculations manifest that the first feature A is related to the quadrupolar transition from the 1 _s_ to 3 _d_ states, while the second feature B is accredited to the dipolar transition from 1 _s_ to the intersite hybridization of O 2 _p_ mediated 

