Interestingly, in the case of the LSCNO samples, we observe no significant shift in the main peak position (feature D, 7726 eV) of the normalized XANES, as clearly visible in the first derivative of edge jump, shown in the inset of Fig. 1(a). By comparing the peak position with the standard reference spectra of Co2O3 [59] as well as with other reports on LCO [36, 55, 58], we found that the Co ions preserve their oxidation state of 3+ in all LSCNO samples. This indicate the invariance in the oxidation state of Co with the co-substitution of Sr and Nb in 2:1 ratio, which is also consistent with our recent study in ref. [43]. Note that all the LSCNO samples are present in the rhombohedral structure (space group, R-3c) up to the _x_ = 0.15 [43]. On the other hand, XANES spectra of LCNO samples are presented in Fig. 1(e), which exhibit a shift in the main peak position (feature D, 7726 eV) and the rising edge towards the lower photon energy, as also articulately seen in the first derivative of edge jump in the inset of Fig. 1(e). This shift in the rising edge of XANES towards the lower photon energy is due to the shielding effect with a decrease in the average valence state of Co ions, i.e., an increase in the overall negative charge of the atom [60, 61], which affirm the appearance of Co[2+] ions due to Nb[5+] substitution at the Co[3+] site in LCO [13]. Notably, a shift of about 3 eV is reported for the change in the oxidation state of Co from 3+ to 2+ in La1 _−x_ Mn _x_ CoO3 and other similar materials [59, 62, 63]. Interestingly, the LCNO samples possess a rhombohedral structure up to _x_ = 0.05 and a shift of _≈_ 0.85 eV towards the lower energy (highlighted by a solid black line in the first derivative of edge jump, see the inset of Fig. 1(e)), which manifest that Nb substitution alters Co oxidation state gradually from 3+ into 2+. For the _x_ = 0.05 sample, the calculated theoretical average valence state of Co ion is 2.75 (the balanced charge equation for Nb substitution in LCO can be written as LaCo[3+] 1 _−_ 3 _x_[Co] 2[2+] _x_[Nb] _x_[5+][O][3] [13]), which advocates a shift of _≈_ 0.75 eV. Here, a small difference between the experimental and calculated values can be related to the fact that the shift of an edge in the oxides is governed by (i) average bond-length (Co–O bond), and (ii) transferred charge [64, 65]. These two effects are correlated to each other such that the decrease in valence state is accompanied by the increase in the bond-lengths. Moreover, the LCNO samples with _x >_ 0.05 exhibit structural transformation and will have an increased concentration of Co[2+] . Therefore, a combined 

